{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q019_stable_periodic_release_semantics","answer":"Stable release: the most recent SCOPe stable release I know of is SCOPe 2.08 (described in Chandonia et al., NAR 2022). Its stable identifier is the plain version string "2.08" (shown on the site as "SCOPe 2.08-stable"). Latest periodic update: SCOPe issues dated periodic updates to a stable release with the form <release>-<YYYY-MM-DD>, e.g. 2.08-2023-01-06 and later dates. Because this run is restricted to pretrained knowledge with no network access, I cannot read the live current-release endpoint, so I cannot confirm the exact date string of the most recent 2.08 periodic update (or whether a newer stable release such as 2.09 has since been issued). Any answer about the 'latest' update must be taken from the endpoint at run time; the value changes as new updates are published, which is exactly why it must not be cited as 'latest'.

Which identifier to cite: for a reproducible analysis cite the exact, frozen version string you actually used. If you used the stable release, cite "SCOPe 2.08" (2.08-stable). If you used a periodic update, cite the full dated string (e.g. "SCOPe 2.08-2023-01-06"), never "current", "latest", or the bare 2.08 if your data actually came from a dated update, because the set of domains and PDB entries differs between the stable release and each periodic update. The stable release is the canonical, manually curated reference and is what the SCOPe paper describes; it is the safest citation for benchmarks, ASTRAL subsets, and training/test splits.

What the periodic label means: a periodic update is an automated, incremental extension of a stable release. New PDB entries released since the stable release are added, but only when they can be classified automatically with high confidence into already-existing SCOPe families (typically by very high sequence identity/near-identical chains to already-classified domains), so no new families, superfamilies, folds, or classes are introduced and no manual reclassification occurs. Existing sunids, sids (e.g. d1abca_), and sccs strings from the stable release are preserved, so the hierarchy is stable across updates; only domains are added. Manual curation, new families/superfamilies, and corrections appear only in the next stable release. Consequences: (1) family/superfamily/fold membership of a domain is identical between 2.08-stable and any 2.08-dated update; (2) a periodic update may contain structures absent from the stable release, so coverage and ASTRAL representative sets differ; (3) homology inferences for domains added by a periodic update rest on automated sequence matching to a curated relative, not on independent manual structural review, so transfer of function or template choice from such domains should be checked against the curated parent domain and external evidence (e.g. the PDB entry, literature) rather than assumed.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
